# REAL lines from the USO MikroTik lab (dropnook/UnraidSecretaryOffice#2), 2026-10-09 (CEST), as Unraid 7.3.3's own
# syslog server (rsyslog, UDP 514, server_filename syslog-%FROMHOST-IP%.log) wrote them on the test server Tower.
# Router: MikroTik RouterOS CHR 7.24.5 (stable), the raw image from mikrotik.com (SHA-256 checked against the download
# page), licence level «free», a KVM VM on isolated libvirt networks only (ether1 «wan» 10.77.1.0/24 with libvirt's DHCP
# as the ISP, ether2 «lan» 10.9.9.0/24 with a second CHR «lab-client» as the fake client, ether3 «mgmt» 10.77.3.0/24 where
# Tower is 10.77.3.1 — Tower's SSH sessions and the syslog path run there). Identity lab-chr, time zone Europe/Zurich.
# Not scrubbed: every address and MAC here is the lab's own (10.77.x, 10.9.9.x, 10.66.0.x, 10.0.0.x, 10.88.0.x,
# 52:54:00:4D:54:xx); users admin, lab (a second full user), alice (L2TP), wan-user (PPPoE); the SSH key is the lab's.
# Lines «uso-step …» are the lab's own markers (/log info), what follows each marker is what that step provoked.
# Lines starting with # are this header (the tests leave them out).
# Action: remote-log-format=default. The router sends no syslog header: rsyslog writes ITS reception time and the
# sender's address as the host (10.77.3.10 — no identity), the topics stay at the start of the text. The step-11 repeat,
# then a clock test: the router's clock set to Sep 30 — the header still says Oct 9 (Unraid's time, not the router's).
